Yes, things are moving fast, with many new laws being _talked about_
but not actually signed into law (yet). (Congress is in recess, of
course.)

It's important for people to get alerted to the general discussion
because this is the period when:

- trial balloons are sent up by Congresscritters

- when Congressbimbos are back in California to listen to their
constituents (in times of yore, the critters lived in their home
districts, and not inside the Beltway)

- this is the time when the NRA and other such groups can vocally
protest the trial balloons

So, here are some of the trial balloons being sent up:

* Clinton, Di-Fi (Diane Feinstein), and others are proposing a ban on
the sale of high-capacity magazines, possibly including confiscation
(how?) of previously bought magazines. The number that is considered
"high-capacity" started at "more than 10" but has also been cited as
"more than 6." Smart shoppers are making sure they have 3 or more mags
for each of their guns. (Point of information: magazines are not
serial numbered (yet?) and no records need be kept of purchase. I had
no problem buying a 30-round clip for my Ruger Mini-14. If the
magazine ban get introduced as a bill, expect the supply to rapidly
dry up.)

As with all of these proposals, this magazine ban could be added to
the upcoming "Omnibus Crime Bill" and passed quickly. (The Omnibus
Crime Bill is getting loaded down with all sorts of "get tough on
crime" riders and additions, as Republicrats of statist persuasion
seek to expand the power of the State while the public is panicked
about subway killers and little girl kidnappers.)

* Moynihan has long argued in his whiskey-besotted way that ammunition
should be heavily taxed, as in 10,000%. This lame idea would have
little effect on muggers and other perps, as they rarely target
practice, but would devastate the upstanding citizen who wished to do
some target practice. Not to mention serious target shooters, hunters,
etc.

This proposal has been endorsed by others and could, on a moment's
notice, be added to the "Omnibus Crime Bill." Hence, wise shoppers
will be purchasing 1000 rounds or more for their guns. (These need not
be the expensive rounds one plans to use for defense, of course. Some
argue that one should only practice with what intends to use, but this
is far too expensive for most of us. I use cheap 9mm ammo--Blazer or
bulk-purchased--for getting in lots of practice.)

* Metzenbaum and others want a total ban on semiautomatic handguns,
and ultimately on all handguns. I give this almost zero chance of
passing, this term. Too many enforcement problems, etc.

But if the public gets panicked enough at violence, Congress may go
this route. Canada has gone this way, as has Europe and most other
countries. Forget the Second Amendment, it's time to disarm the
population.

A downside of this for would-be gun buyers is that confiscation is
likely to be withought any compensation, or at a price set by the
bureaucrats. Thus, my H&K SP-89 "assault pistol," which I paid $1000
for several years ago and which is now selling for $2500 (due to the
ban on further imports of it and similar guns), will get confisated.
And of course the accessories that go with it, like lasers ($200),
carrying cases, ammunition, etc., effectively become worthless with
confiscation of the gun, so the financial loss is even greater.

The postive aspect is that gun confiscation would likely provoke
massive civil disobedience, the "fight to the death" of at least some
martyrs (probably not me, though), many lawsuits and injunctions
filed, and a generally loud debate.

So, these are the main proposals in the air today.

Any of them could quickly be railroaded into the Omnibus Crime Bill
and then passed. What I _expect_ to happen is this: a ban on
high-capacity magazines, but not the guns themselves. Existing guns
would be sold only with 6- or 10-round magazines, as in Canada today,
and possession of a larger capacity magazine would be treated as a
misdemeanor (even I can't see the State sending someone to prison for
several years for illegal possesssion of a 15-round magazine, not when
they're letting out kidnappers and murderers early to make room in the
prisons).

It's conceivable that target ranges would be required to inspect
pistols to make sure no illegal magazines are in use. Maybe even to
report the owners....Big Brother on the target range.

Here's a summary of actions most of you should consider:

- If you want a handgun, buy it as soon as possible.

- Buy it at a gun show, with no paperwork, if your region allows it
(No longer allowed in California, for example). Pay cash and take
immediate delivery.

- If you want a semiautomatic (a pistol), act now!

- Your gun probably comes with 2 magazines. Get a third one.
(Magazines can wear out, feed lips can get bent, etc.)

- Buy at least 500 rounds of ammo, with at least 250 rounds used for
familiarization and practice, then at least 50 per year for continuing
practice. (This won't last long, hence my recommendation of 1000 or
more rounds.)

Ask not for whom the bell tolls; it tolls for thee.


--Tim May


