You cite a 3-4% return on rental property in the Bay Area, but
it is well known that it is one of the most expensive areas in
the country for housing.  Rental rates are more constrained than
sale prices, because people buy rent every month, while on 
average they buy houses only every 7 years.  Thus, when housing
prices have risen to higher than average levels, the return on
rental property would be expected to be low.

Here in the Houston market, the reverse holds true.  Houston
has depressed housing prices due to the oil bust.  A new  1700 SF
house sells for 100,000, while the rent is 11,000 per year.  After
property taxes, the net is 8500 per year.  And that's just quoting
an example on the street this office building is on.  With some
hunting for good deals, you should be able to better this a bit.

Even if you use my example, with a 50,000 mortgage at 7.5% you
will incur 3750 in interest, leaving 4750 cash flow on
50000 investment yielding 9.5% return.

