	A thought which struck me recently:

	A lot of near-future dreams of some form of network connecting
everyone up (via cable, or telephone, or what-not) usually have
something like a Nintendo or a Sega or a 3DO or an Atari Jaguar as the
'home station.' (a fair number of dreamers having given up on the idea of
everyone having a 'real' workstation at home..)

	I was thinking about what's going to be running on these boxes
-- what they're going to have going for them to make people willing to
plunk down the 2-3-4 hundred dollars, and see them as essential as a
VCR if not more so.  No idea -- I suppose a fair amount of tie-in with
entertainment stuff's a fair guess.  With 0x0200 cable channels available, 
TV Guide'll end up looking like a phone book.  But that's easy. 

	What really struck me is that there are some significant
similarities between the vision of the Newton-style palmtop computers
and these home computer/appliance things.  They're seen as consumer
devices, ideal price is under $500 (I've heard $300 in some talks).
Coincidentally, the Newton and Panasonic's 3D0 incarnation both came
out with a SRP of $700.  They're both much better as information
receivers than information producers, since their input methods are
fairly slow.  As a consequence, they both will win a lot when networks
are in their respective operating environments (be it wirelessland, or
homewiredland) And they've both been met by a fair amount of media
skepticism when first released.

	Comments?  

